{
  "_comment": "F-16A Block 10/15 geometry STATION COORDINATES, extracted from Brandt's 3-view drawing tables. Units: ft throughout. x runs aft from the nose. y is spanwise, z is vertical. Read this file's _note keys before use: the fuselage and nacelle blocks are true cross-section stations, but the lifting-surface blocks are PLANFORM OUTLINES and carry no thickness.",
  "_source": "VnV/BrandtF16A/GroundTruth/Brandt F-16A - corrections.xls, sheet 'Geom', read 2026-08-19 with MATLAB readcell.",
  "_extracted_by": "Claude, 2026-08-19",
  "_scope_note": {
    "cross_section_stations": "fuselage, nacelle. Each row gives width and height at a station.",
    "planform_outlines": "wing, pitch_ctrl, strake, vert_tail, aileron_elevon, le_flap, te_flap. Each row is a planform CORNER, not a section. There is NO thickness here, so these alone cannot give a wetted area. Take t/c from examples/F16A/inputs/f16a_L3.json, or from the workbook's 'Wing Airfoil' / 'Stab Airfoil' sheets.",
    "area_distribution": "The per-component cross-sectional area at 20 stations. This is what Raymer 6th ed. Fig. 7.38, p. 207 integrates for volume, and what the area-ruled Amax uses."
  },
  "_convention_notes": {
    "y_sign": "NOT consistent in the workbook. wing and pitch_ctrl put y_left NEGATIVE. strake, aileron_elevon, le_flap and te_flap put y_left POSITIVE. Copied verbatim.",
    "z_sign_fuselage": "z_bottom > z_top numerically, so height = z_bottom - z_top. The centreline is the mean of the two, and it equals .fuselage.frames[].z_ft in f16a_geometry.json.",
    "vert_tail_top_view": "Degenerate: every y is 0, because the fin is one panel on the centreline. Its shape is in x_side_ft / z_side_ft.",
    "repeated_apex": "Each planform outline closes back on its apex, so 'Apex' appears twice."
  },
